{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLp97DPJRrFdQGrM98uU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`Pow.as_real_imag` no longer claims a zero imaginary part when the base might be zero and the exponent is a negative integer. For `1/Sum(1, (p, 8, 6))`, it now returns `(re(1/Sum(1, (p, 8, 6))), im(1/Sum(1, (p, 8, 6))))`. It used to return `(1/Sum(...), 0)`.\n\nI ran `sympy/core/tests/test_power.py`, `test_expr.py` and `sympy/functions/elementary/tests/test_complexes.py`. They gave 183 passed and 5 xfailed. I didn't add a new test for this case.\n\nA symbol known to be real but possibly zero, such as `1/Symbol('y', real=True)`, now returns `(re(1/y), im(1/y))` instead of `(1/y, 0)`. A real nonzero symbol still returns `(1/z, 0)`.\n\nThe change is a few lines in `sympy/core/power.py`, in the integer-exponent branch. It also returns `None` when `re`/`im` call back in with `ignore` set to the expression itself, as the existing fallback at the end of that function does. Without that, `re(self)` and `im(self)` recursed forever."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":667,"cache_read_input_tokens":22953,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":667},"output_tokens":408,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2215b7db-ac27-43c5-ae6a-7816b7bd65d2","uuid":"970439e8-50bd-4f54-a9ac-5c8aadef7018","timestamp":"2026-11-24T00:33:52.033Z","request_id":"req_011ChLp966fpGAngDuotSja2"}
